-- title:
--   Existence of a Fricke automorphism at level p²
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime. Write $jq$ for the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$ as a Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$, and for $d \ge 1$ let $\mathrm{qExpand}\ \mathbb{Q}\ d$ be the ring endomorphism of $\mathbb{Q}$-Laurent series obtained by multiplying all exponents by $d$ (substitution $q \mapsto q^{d}$), so that $\mathrm{qExpand}\ \mathbb{Q}\ d\ jq$ is the series $j(q^{d})$. For $N \ge 1$, `modularFunctionFieldFull N` is the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the set of all $j(q^{d})$ with $d \ge 1$ and $d \mid N$. The theorem asserts, for $N = p \cdot p$, the existence of a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of `modularFunctionFieldFull (p * p)` satisfying the predicate `IsFrickeAutFull (p * p)`, that is: for every pair of positive naturals $a, b$ with $a b = p^{2}$, $\sigma$ carries the element $j(q^{a})$ of the field (taken with its canonical membership witness, $a$ being a divisor of $p^2$) to the element $j(q^{b})$. Concretely, $\sigma$ interchanges $j(q)$ and $j(q^{p^{2}})$ and fixes $j(q^{p})$. Only existence is asserted; no uniqueness and no relation $\sigma^{2} = \mathrm{id}$ is part of the conclusion.
--
--   This is the Fricke involution $w_{p^{2}}$ at level $p^{2}$, realised purely in terms of $q$-expansions on the function field $\mathbb{Q}(j, j_{p}, j_{p^{2}})$ of the full modular curve of level $p^{2}$. It is the existence statement from which the named automorphism `frickeInvolutionFull (p * p)` is obtained, [`ModularCurve.isFrickeAutFull_frickeInvolutionFull_sq`](thm.html#ModularCurve.isFrickeAutFull_frickeInvolutionFull_sq) recording that the chosen automorphism indeed has the stated effect on the generators.
